WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.067 --> 00:00:01.177
(cheers and applause)

00:00:01.177 --> 00:00:02.690
- [Kelly] Oh my God, I love this!

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.799
- Thank you.
- [Kelly] You look so great!

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:05.996
- Thank you, you too!

00:00:05.996 --> 00:00:08.280
- You're all glowy.
- Thank you.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.630
- Congratulations on all the things

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.770
happening in your life right now.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.869
You're just killing it.
(cheers and applause)

00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:14.730
You're killing it.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.810
Last time we talked,

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.280
it's Christmas time. Last time we talked

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.380
about Christmas villages,
like we decorated,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.520
Have you decorated this year?

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.260
- I'm halfway up.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.670
My house was under renovation,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.400
so everything was in storage.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.450
They pulled it down,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.040
and I started putting my tree together

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.620
and I was like, "Something's missing."

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.060
My team thinks I'm crazy

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.680
'cause I have a lot of villages,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.730
a lot of houses.
- [Kelly] Me too.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.200
- I'm like, "Something's missing."

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.940
And she was like, "You're right!"

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.650
My sister had everything pulled down,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:41.977
and I was like, "I told you!

00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:44.160
"The house was missing there."

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.810
I know my village!

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.580
- [Kelly] Me too.
- What did you add this year?

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.770
- So, my son decided,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.670
because we have New York, we have islands,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.553
we have New York and Brooklyn.

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:55.386
- Whoa!

00:00:55.386 --> 00:00:56.219
- Yeah, we have a Brooklyn scene.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:57.697
I was like, "Okay." I was like,

00:00:57.697 --> 00:00:58.980
"Maybe next year we'll add Jersey."

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.480
I don't know what we're doing.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.770
They're all the Charles Dickens.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.090
It's still the department,

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.650
it's still that old school thing.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.490
But he calls them,

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:06.323
my 7-year-old son was like,

00:01:06.323 --> 00:01:07.477
"Oh no, that's Brooklyn."

00:01:07.477 --> 00:01:08.857
(laughter)
I was like,

00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:10.897
"I didn't even know you knew that yet."

00:01:10.897 --> 00:01:11.760
"I didn't even know you,"
(laughing)

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.170
like, "What?"

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.700
But yeah, it's all set.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.380
We're not final. It's not final.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.220
This week is actually,

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.240
I'm putting the final touches.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.340
- When I get back, that's
when it's all together.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.510
I'm adding a road.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.520
I added a carousel.
- [Kelly] We have a road.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.048
- A beautiful carousel.
(gasps)

00:01:25.048 --> 00:01:27.600
- We don't have a carousel.
- I added an ice skate rink.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.680
- We have an ice skating rink.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.360
- Do you have the water fountain?

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.414
- Girl, what? No!

00:01:32.414 --> 00:01:33.631
We don't have a water fountain!

00:01:33.631 --> 00:01:35.003
- I have the water fountain.

00:01:35.003 --> 00:01:35.836
- I don't have a water fountain

00:01:35.836 --> 00:01:36.707
and I don't have a carousel.

00:01:36.707 --> 00:01:38.741
- [Taraji] I have a police
station and a fire station.

00:01:38.741 --> 00:01:39.660
- We have a police
station and fire station.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.730
- Does the car come in and out like this?

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.875
- Shut up!
(laughter)

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:45.708
(laughing)

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:46.850
I'm gonna need a picture.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.500
Okay, so what about your childhood?

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.270
What's like a favorite Christmas memory?

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.920
- Oh, favorite Christmas memory.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.450
I remember one year,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.280
because of me, it was my bright idea.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.590
I'm the creative in the family.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:01:59.917
I put together, I was like,

00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:03.870
"Let's have a family talent show."

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.150
And so, I thought I was gonna win

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.920
because, you know, I'm
the talent of the family.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.240
(laughter)

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.520
I sang, um, "Angel"

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.833
by um, uh,

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:14.850
♪ Angel ♪

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.435
Anita Baker! God, I drew a blank.

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:17.970
Thanks!

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.920
Then my uncles came in

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.960
and they blew the roof off the house

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.360
with the Temptations song.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.563
- Oh, which one?

00:02:26.638 --> 00:02:29.530
♪ It's Merry Christmas and Happy New, ♪

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.170
Cause I have an uncle with the very,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.390
why can't I think of songs today?

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:34.230
Say that again.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.930
Silent Night, yes! Silent Night.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.040
- No one's ever heard of it.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:38.873
- No, right.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.490
It's a new song apparently.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.620
- So they kicked your behind.
- They killed it.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.330
They totally stole the show.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.010
Literally, we talk about it to this day.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.050
I mean, this was when I was 12.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.490
I'm 53. We're still talking

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.610
about this moment.
- Oh my God, wait.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.400
Wait, I totally thought you meant

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.530
you did this as an adult.
- No!

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:56.363
- And you were like,

00:02:56.363 --> 00:02:57.667
"I wanted everyone to do a talent show

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:58.620
"'cause I was gonna win."
- [Taraji] I did.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.840
- I was just picturing you at like 35,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.539
just like, I've got my Anita Baker.

00:03:02.539 --> 00:03:03.372
(laughing)

00:03:03.372 --> 00:03:04.620
Okay, I love it.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.360
It would've been better
if you would've been 35.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.470
So what's one of your favorite

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.880
Christmas memories with your son?

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:09.870
- [Taraji] Oh, God!

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:10.800
- He's older now, but,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.690
- No, he's grown now, but,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.970
I loved when they're little bits.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.950
When they're little,
little, little, because,

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.390
but you know, that's my baby.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.250
He's so grown now.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.967
I produced that play on Broadway,

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:23.170
"Jaja's African Hair Braiding."

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:24.874
(applause and cheering)

00:03:24.874 --> 00:03:25.707
- [Kelly] That's so awesome,

00:03:25.707 --> 00:03:26.990
to see his mama killing it.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.040
- Yes, he was so proud.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.600
He really was proud.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:31.770
- I have the young age still,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.477
so it is kind of still fun.
- It's fun!

00:03:34.477 --> 00:03:36.990
You know, I think we have
more fun than they do,

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.840
like putting the toys
together the night before.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.130
But my son was funny.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.260
He liked what he liked.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.360
He would pick like two or three things.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.770
I swear, I think I picked the wrong

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.270
baby up at the hospital.
(laughter)

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.430
But he would pick like four toys,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.840
and that's all he wanted.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.707
I would buy him like 12, thinking,

00:03:53.707 --> 00:03:55.110
"He wants this, he wants this."

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.450
- This is me with my children.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.410
- He hurt my feelings,

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.147
because I would watch him open the gifts

00:04:00.147 --> 00:04:02.580
and he would be like, "Eh, oh."

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.280
He would get those four
gifts that he asked for,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.140
grab those up and go in his room,

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.000
and all the other stuff
I bought is just sitting

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.890
outside and under the tree.
- I can beat this.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.090
I can beat this.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.239
My kids don't even open them all.

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:15.870
(laughter)

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.580
There's not that many.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:18.810
You know what I'm saying?

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:19.890
They will literally open,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:20.910
once they find something they like,

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:21.743
they just go play with it.
- [Taraji] They're gone.

00:04:21.743 --> 00:04:23.377
- I'm like, "I'm sorry.

00:04:23.377 --> 00:04:26.250
"I spent a lot of time doing this."

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.632
- [Taraji] Exactly.
- Yes.

00:04:27.632 --> 00:04:29.010
(laughing)

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.540
- These kids, they're so privileged.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.620
- I'm like, "What?"

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.880
They do not know what it's like.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:33.870
Anyway.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.510
And we worked hard for that, but whatever.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:36.343
(cheering and applause)

00:04:36.343 --> 00:04:39.840
That was a look at her new
movie, The Color Purple.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.890
That is an intense,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:41.737
I was telling her, I was like,

00:04:41.737 --> 00:04:43.629
"My thighs hurt from watching this clip."

00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:44.462
- Yes!
(laughter)

00:04:44.462 --> 00:04:45.990
- You're on that table. It looks fun.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.400
Sometimes when you have alcohol in you,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.140
you don't know until the next day.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.240
But when you don't have alcohol in you,

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:52.950
it feels like you're snowboarding.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:54.540
Yeah, your thighs burn.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.700
- No, everything was burning.
(laughter)

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.800
And what you did not see is that I was,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.440
I had to ice my knees
in between every take.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.330
- How many takes? How many times a day?

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:04.200
- Listen,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.810
Oprah has seen all of the footage

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.610
from every angle, and she counted.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.232
She said I climbed up
on that table 88 times.

00:05:11.232 --> 00:05:13.140
(audience wooing)

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.730
But your adrenaline goes.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:15.930
I wasn't counting.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.850
- I don't think so, no.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.770
- 88 times.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:20.970
- [Kelly] No.
- [Taraji] And I don't have

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.890
Meg the Stallion knees.
(laughter)

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.120
I'm climbing up there

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.189
and I'm dropping it low, child.

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:31.189
(audience wooing) (applause)

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:34.969
- I was like, "Woo!"

00:05:34.969 --> 00:05:37.050
That right there, in
the heels, by the way.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.148
- Yeah, they were character shoes though.

00:05:39.148 --> 00:05:40.410
They were character shoes.
- Who cares?

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:41.340
They're not flat.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.740
- They're not flat.
You're absolutely right.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:44.820
No, it was a lot of work.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.620
- What was it like for you?

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.660
I can't imagine seeing this as a kid

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.030
and then now you're in it, right?

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.773
- I never imagined myself in it.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.040
I just remember seeing the movie in 1985.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.420
I was 15,

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.673
and I had been,

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.540
that's when the acting bug awakened in me

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.190
and I was like, "I think
I want to do that."

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.020
I remember going to the theater and seeing

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.350
all those black faces, and I saw me.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.090
That allowed me to dream.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.180
That allowed me to go,
"Hey, I can do that."

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.620
And so shortly after that,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.690
I started really getting
serious about acting.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.730
Then when I got into
college, I read the book

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.400
and I was like, "Whoo."

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.360
I mean, you know,

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.100
Alice Walker did a great thing.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.160
This is like one of the cornerstone

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.880
pieces of literature of American culture.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.380
Everybody knows this movie,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.670
white, black, it doesn't matter.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:38.432
It transcends race, color, religion.

00:06:38.432 --> 00:06:39.600
- It's a phenomenon.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.490
- It is, and everybody

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.890
can really see themselves
in some of these characters.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.020
What I love about it is, with this film,

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.380
we're gonna be breaking
generational curses,

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.930
healing families,

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.070
hopefully.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.923
Because a lot of us were
healed doing this film.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.520
You think you got over some things, and

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.860
here you are faced with
a character that has,

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.570
and then you have to kind of take the

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.020
bandaid off of something
you thought that healed.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.150
Art can be really healing, you know?

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:10.560
It really can.
- [Kelly] Did you dive

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:11.827
into the character? Did
you relate it at all?

00:07:11.827 --> 00:07:13.560
(applause)

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.000
- You know,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.430
I did, because she was literally

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.683
the cookie of the 1920s.

00:07:19.683 --> 00:07:21.330
- [Kelly] I know!
- [Taraji] No, literally,

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.017
she got off the plantation and was like,

00:07:23.017 --> 00:07:24.180
"I'm gonna make something of myself."

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.210
You gotta remember, this film, this movie,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.500
the book opens up,

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.930
it's the first generation of freed slaves.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.107
So a lot of slaves was like,

00:07:32.107 --> 00:07:34.560
"Well, we free, but where we going?"

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.500
They were still sharecropping and minding

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.180
the land that they were living on.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.120
This one decided, I'm going to break free

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.650
and I'm gonna make something of myself.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.630
And so, she was a change maker.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.090
Every time she came on a
scene, she lifted this,

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.980
she lifted it, and everybody,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:50.813
I had a journalist say,

00:07:50.813 --> 00:07:52.297
"She's almost like an alien

00:07:52.297 --> 00:07:54.420
"that comes down from outer space."

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.800
And that's how the town treats her.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:57.360
It's like a big deal when Shug Avery

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.420
comes to town because

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:02.310
the people that she left
behind didn't have dreams,

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.060
or if they did, they were deferred.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.900
She didn't allow her
dreams to be deferred.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:07.830
- Yeah, she was afraid,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.090
people were afraid to dream that big.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.560
- Exactly.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.660
She was the dreamer,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.610
and she came back and changed lives.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.900
- I feel like this is you.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.340
I feel like you're that person too.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.140
Every time I run into
you, you're such a light.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.100
Every time you come,
(Taraji gasps)

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.360
You are that person though.
- Thank you.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.058
- When you enter a room,
(applause)

00:08:23.058 --> 00:08:24.787
- Thank you.
- It couldn't have been cast

00:08:24.787 --> 00:08:25.811
more perfectly.

00:08:25.811 --> 00:08:27.930
- [Taraji] Thank you.

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:30.150
- When it comes to talking about injustice

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.010
or racism, things like that,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.360
I have friends,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.130
they have to talk to
their kids differently

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:36.600
than I had to have a talk with my kids.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.280
Did you feel that with
your boy growing up?

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.780
- Oh, absolutely.
- [Kelly] Even now, at 30.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:40.643
- Oh, even to this day.

00:08:40.643 --> 00:08:44.640
I mean, I think any black
mother, grandmother,

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.570
niece, auntie, we worry
about our black men.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.430
We worry about,

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.680
if they leave the house,
will they come back?

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.090
It's something that lives
in our subconscious.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:58.350
It's just there.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.900
We carry it well, but it's always there.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:02.160
Especially when you're triggered

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.130
by something on the
news or you're scrolling

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:08.640
Instagram and some kid
has been unjustly arrested

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.230
or shot or something.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.510
It sends a shock wave,
not only through you,

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:13.950
but through the entire community

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:15.270
because we feel it.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:16.920
We feel it as one unit.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.341
Am I lying?
- [Audience Member] No!

00:09:18.341 --> 00:09:20.400
(Taraji laughing) (applause)

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.230
And also, even as a,

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:23.880
I mean, I can't imagine
the next step of that.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.040
But even as a parent,
you always worry anyway.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.050
- [Taraji] Always.
- There's that fear anyway.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:29.940
You want 'em to fly, you want 'em to soar,

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.190
you want 'em to do stuff.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:33.510
I'm afraid of people sometimes

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.553
outside my house.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:09:35.553 --> 00:09:38.280
It's just that extra layer of,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.590
will he be protected by the law?

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:41.423
You know?

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:43.530
- Man.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:44.850
- It's tough, but, I don't,

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.050
- I love that he has,

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.640
y'all are sitting there though,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.320
with that playbill, and it's your show,

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.480
and you're doing it. I just love,

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.520
to show him what a successful

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:54.823
boss mom looks like.

00:09:54.823 --> 00:09:56.636
- Oh, he's very proud.
(applause)

00:09:56.636 --> 00:09:58.023
- I know, but that's like,

00:09:58.023 --> 00:10:00.060
'cause you've also set the bar

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.890
for whatever relationships he's gonna have

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.510
in the future. You know what I'm saying?

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:05.100
I don't think we realize that

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:05.970
until you become a parent.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.177
You're like, "Oh man,
we're setting the bar

00:10:07.177 --> 00:10:08.917
"for our kids. They're watching us.

00:10:08.917 --> 00:10:10.057
"I wanna show 'em what love looks like,

00:10:10.057 --> 00:10:11.137
"what success looks like,

00:10:11.137 --> 00:10:12.480
"what happiness looks like."

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.920
You're doing such a great job.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.267
- Thank you so much.
(applause)

00:10:15.267 --> 00:10:16.100
- [Kelly] It's so cool.
- [Taraji] Thank you.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.107
(laughing) Thank you.
(cheers and applause)

00:10:18.107 --> 00:10:19.007
- Anyway, I know that you like games.

00:10:19.007 --> 00:10:20.340
I know that you're competitive.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.110
I'm competitive, so I feel like we

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.789
should play a Christmas game.
- Okay.

00:10:23.789 --> 00:10:24.670
- Okay.
(cheers and applause)

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.117
Well we've got a Christmas
game coming up next.

00:10:26.117 --> 00:10:27.960
Y'all, The Color Purple's in theaters

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.160
starting Christmas day.
We'll be right back.

